# File lines (ADR 0010), against an echo server: look at "data" in the response
# (or "form" for the form bodies).
#
# A File line is `<` or `<@` at the very start of a line, then a space or a
# tab, then the path. `<` sends the file's bytes as they are; `<@` fills in
# {{variables}} in it. The files are in ./files, none ending in a line break.
#
# Run file: httpbin.org rate-limits (429) a burst of requests, so set the
# Runner's Delay to about 1000 ms, or point @echo at a local httpbin
# (docker run -p 8080:80 kennethreitz/httpbin, then http://localhost:8080/anything).
# The last request fails on purpose: turn off "Stop on first failure" or it's
# simply where the run ends.

@who = HttpDesk
@echo = https://httpbin.org/anything

### 1. Whole-file body (unchanged): sends payload.json as it is
POST {{echo}}/whole-file
Content-Type: application/json

< ./files/payload.json

### 2. Whole-file body with <@: sends "Hello from HttpDesk"
POST {{echo}}/whole-file-variables
Content-Type: text/plain

<@ ./files/greeting.txt

### 3. A File line among other lines: sends "before\nthe spliced file\nafter"
POST {{echo}}/splice
Content-Type: text/plain

before
< ./files/note.txt
after

### 4. File lines at the start and the end, and two of them
# Sends "the spliced file\nmiddle: HttpDesk\nthe spliced file"
POST {{echo}}/start-and-end
Content-Type: text/plain

< ./files/note.txt
middle: {{who}}
< ./files/note.txt

### 5. `<` keeps the file's {{ }}; `<@` fills them in
# Sends "raw: Hello from {{who}}\nfilled: Hello from HttpDesk"
POST {{echo}}/raw-vs-filled
Content-Type: text/plain

raw:
< ./files/greeting.txt
filled:
<@ ./files/greeting.txt

### 6. Spliced into JSON: the "json" field of the reply shows the parsed array
POST {{echo}}/json
Content-Type: application/json

[
< ./files/payload.json
,
< ./files/item.json
]

### 7. Spliced into XML; the multi-line start tag is content, with no warning
POST {{echo}}/xml
Content-Type: application/xml

<pets
  xmlns="urn:demo">
< ./files/fragment.xml
</pets>

### 8. A tab after `<`, and spaces after the path: both still File lines
POST {{echo}}/tab-and-trailing-spaces
Content-Type: text/plain

tab:
<	./files/note.txt
trailing spaces:
< ./files/note.txt   

### 9. No space after `<`: sent as the text "<./files/payload.json", with a warning
POST {{echo}}/no-space
Content-Type: application/json

<./files/payload.json

### 10. Indented: sent as text, with a warning
POST {{echo}}/indented
Content-Type: application/json

  < ./files/payload.json

### 11. Form body with a File line: sent like a text body, no `&` joining
# Comment and #~ lines are left out, so the disabled field isn't sent.
# Sends "a=1\nthe spliced file"; httpbin reads it as the form field a = "1\nthe spliced file".
POST {{echo}}/form-with-file
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

a=1
# a comment
#~ &disabled=yes
< ./files/note.txt

### 12. Form body without File lines (unchanged): sends "a=1&b=2"
POST {{echo}}/form
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

a=1
&b=2
#~ &disabled=yes

### 13. Multipart file part (unchanged): uploads upload.txt
POST {{echo}}/multipart
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=B

--B
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="title"

{{who}}
--B
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="file"; filename="upload.txt"
Content-Type: text/plain

< ./files/upload.txt
--B--

### 14. Disabled body: its File line isn't read, so the missing file isn't reported
# Turn the body on in the Body tab to see the error appear.
POST {{echo}}/disabled-body
Content-Type: text/plain
#~| before
#~| < ./files/does-not-exist.txt

### 15. Missing file among other lines: an error in the editor, and Send is blocked
POST {{echo}}/missing
Content-Type: text/plain

before
< ./files/does-not-exist.txt
after
